Once upon a time, in the bustling streets of Stranglethorn Vale, a Gnome Warlock named Groggly was on a quest to slay the fearsome Shadowfang. His trusty companion, a succubus named Sylph, was by his side, her seductive charm and shadowy powers a constant source of amusement and utility.

As they ventured deeper into the jungle, Groggly found himself facing a horde of skeletal warriors. The battle was fierce, and the skeletal horde was relentless. Groggly, relying on his warlock abilities, summoned a voidwalker, a creature of shadow and torment. The voidwalker, with its taunt and threat generation, proved invaluable in keeping the skeletal horde at bay, allowing Groggly to focus on the more delicate matters of shadow magic.

Meanwhile, Sylph, with her seduction and lesser invisibility, managed to charm a few of the skeletal warriors, turning them against their own kind. However, as the battle raged on, Groggly realized that the voidwalker's consume shadows ability was proving more useful than Sylph's soothing kiss, as it allowed him to regenerate his health out of combat, keeping him in the fight longer.

As the battle drew to a close, Groggly and Sylph emerged victorious, their bond strengthened by the trials they had faced together. But as they rested, Groggly couldn't help but wonder why he had chosen to summon a voidwalker over Sylph, despite her more versatile abilities. It was then that he realized that, in the heat of battle, the voidwalker's unique abilities made it a more effective tool for their current quest.

Answer: Most warlocks are seen with voidwalkers because they are better suited for high-threat situations, such as boss fights, where their torment and threat generation abilities are crucial. While succubi are excellent for leveling and questing due to their versatility and crowd control abilities, voidwalkers provide a unique advantage in high-stakes encounters. [end of text]


